      "value": "@{5acad9ecc9dba010c9c3ec68|full_name}: I am. They're basically the best option available right now. But this leads into two issues:\n1. Lets say I want a toggle pattern where enabling one item disables another item. e.g. I have a \"slot\" which is a \"shirt\" and if I enable shirt2 in the menu while having shirt1 on, I was shirt1 to switch to off automatically. This requires me to use a VRC Avatar Parameter Driver for each behavior like this, which is extra effort compared to just have them on the same int parameter.\n2. If I have between 5 to 7 items on the same logical \"slot\", using 5-7 bools is more expensive than had I been able to use a single int with a cost of 4. This is also true for 8 to 15 items on the same slot, where I end up using int8, but an int4 would suffice.\nRealistically, I have between 5 and 10 items on each slot on my avatars on average (the way I got to this number is based on mesh/texture resources sizes and trying to keep the avatars under a reasonable 50MB download size which is the default avatar download safety gate in the VRChat client), which means I either use a lot of bools or an int that costs 8 for each \"slot\". an int with a cost of 4 would allow up to 16 states per slot, which would be a lot less expensive overall.\nBeing able to replace 5-7 bool setups or int8 setups with int4 for each would open up a lot of parameter memory which I could then use for sliders (tail length slider, eye color hue slider, etc), all of which have no price when it comes to avatar download size since it's just parameter adjustments, so I'd like to have those.\nBasically, an int (and float if possible) with a price of 4 would allow avatar creators to have a lot more dynamic avatars and an easier creation process at no cost for the client/server.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
