      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{5e44a1bd0c91c2477a8b8acb|full_name}: I mean I get your point that I'm doing more with my avatar than what has been the standard for most users for quite a while, but since there are systems in place to allow it, why not use them?\nMy goal is to optimize and reduce redundancy and unnecessary repetitions. I see no reason that people would need to download the same avatar assets multiple times if you can simply have parameter toggles on one avatar. Less downloads is friendlier for both clients and the server bandwidth.\nThis will open up possibilities for people to make more interesting avatars over time, and honestly, isn't this a really small and easy change to implement? There is already code there to work with Int64bit/8byte, just dupe the same code and support Int32/4bit, I imagine this is less than one workday for one developer.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2021-04-19T02:22:00.176Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "128 total memory when each int takes up 8 memory is too limiting if you want to have many toggles on your avatar for purposes of organization (hat, glasses, earrings, mask, shirt, belt, etc... you run out very fast).\nWhy do we only get int64? why can't we have access to int32 which only takes up 4 memory? it would make organizing toggles way better.\n(would be nice to have float32 if that exists too for hue sliders and such)",
